# Untitled

Metal-organic frameworks-derived nitrogen-doped carbon supported nanostructured PtNi catalyst for enhanced hydrosilylation of 1-octene
Junfeng Wen\(^{1,2}\), Yuanjun Chen\(^{2}\), Shufang Ji\(^{2}\) (✉), Jian Zhang\(^{2}\), Dingsheng Wang\(^{2}\), and Yadong Li\(^{2}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\) School of chemistry and chemical engineering, Yulin University, Yulin 719000, China  \(^{2}\) Department of Chemistry, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2019  Received: 17 June 2019 / Revised: 21 July 2019 / Accepted: 23 July 2019
## ABSTRACT
Here, we successfully developed nanostructured PtNi particles supported on nitrogen-doped carbon (NC), which were obtained by carbonization of metal-organic frameworks under different temperatures, forming the nano-PtNi/NC-600, nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900 and nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalysts. For hydrosilylation of 1-octene, we found that the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst exhibits higher activity for anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation of 1-octene than those of nano-PtNi/NC-600, nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900 catalysts. Experiments have verified that benefiting from obvious charge transfer from nano-PtNi particles to NC support carbonized at \(1,000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst achieved almost complete conversion and produce exclusive adduct for anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation of 1-octene. Importantly, the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst exhibited good reusability for the hydrosilylation reaction. This work provides a new path to optimize electronic structure of catalysts by support modification to enhance electron transfer between metal active species and supports for highly catalytic performance.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, nanostructured PtNi particles, hydrosilylation, nitrogen-doped carbon
## 1 Introduction
Metal-catalyzed alkene hydrosilylation, the addition of silicon and hydrogen unit across a C–C double bond to build Si–C bond for forming alkylsilanes, is an important and useful reaction in silicon industry because of its widespread use in large-scale production of diverse commodities such as silicone rubbers, molding products, paper release coatings, and pressure-sensitive adhesives [1–6]. For decades, noble metal complexes, especially the homogeneous Pt complexes, have attracted extensive research interest and industrial attention for hydrosilylation, owing to their excellent activity and selectively [7–10]. However, the disposable consumption of platinum complexes and undesired isomerization of final products usually gives rise to the subsequent high-cost, appalling waste of scarce platinum resources, and energy-intensive purification and treatment steps [11, 12]. Therefore, developing heterogeneous Pt-based catalysts are of great value and practical significance. Great research efforts have been devoted to developing efficient and recyclable heterogeneous Pt-based catalysts [5, 12, 13]. Unfortunately, these heterogeneous catalysts have some shortcomings, including unsatisfactory catalytic activity and selectivity, and limited stability, which hinder their large-scale practical application. Currently, it is urgent but remains a grand challenge to develop remarkable heterogeneous catalysts with high performance and good stability for hydrosilylation reaction.Heterogeneous catalysts composed of metal sites and supports play a key role in various important chemical and industrial processes [14–16]. For heterogeneous catalysis, the electronic structure of metal sites has an important influence on the activity and selectivity [17, 18]. Therefore, tuning the electronic structure of metal sites are one of important approaches to enhance the performance. It is well accepted that slight structure changes of supports can have an underestimated impact on the catalytic performance in the nanometre scale [19]. If the electronic state of the metal sites can be appropriately controlled by modification of the supports, it will be expected to enhance the activity and selectivity of heterogeneous catalysis [17]. However, modifying structure of supports is also a great challenge due to the design limitation of controllable and variable supports. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a novel class of versatile crystalline materials owing to their tailorable structure, tunable chemical composition and adjustable functionality [20–26]. And, with different thermal treatment, the structural difference of nitrogen doped porous carbon (NC) matrix derived from MOFs is clearly obvious [27–39]. This provides a potential opportunity to study the effect of supports on performance of supported metal catalysts, even though it is not yet realized.Herem, we designed the fabrication of nanostructured platinum-nickel particles supported on nitrogen-doped porous carbons (nano-PtNi/NC), which were derived from zeolite-imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) under thermal treatment with different temperatures. For hydrosilylation of 1-octene, nano-PtNi/NC-1000 can achieve almost complete conversion and produce exclusive adduct for anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation of 1-octene. The highly efficient and selective catalysis are mainly attributed to the obvious charge transfer between nano-PtNi and nitrogen-doped porous carbon support, leading to the electronic structure of platinum species with partial charge. This catalyst also exhibits excellent reusability towards anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation.
Heterogeneous catalysts composed of metal sites and supports play a key role in various important chemical and industrial processes [14–16]. For heterogeneous catalysis, the electronic structure of metal sites has an important influence on the activity and selectivity [17, 18]. Therefore, tuning the electronic structure of metal sites are
one of important approaches to enhance the performance. It is well accepted that slight structure changes of supports can have an underestimated impact on the catalytic performance in the nanometre scale [19]. If the electronic state of the metal sites can be appropriately controlled by modification of the supports, it will be expected to enhance the activity and selectivity of heterogeneous catalysis [17]. However, modifying structure of supports is also a great challenge due to the design limitation of controllable and variable supports. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a novel class of versatile crystalline materials owing to their tailorable structure, tunable chemical composition and adjustable functionality [20–26]. And, with different thermal treatment, the structural difference of nitrogen doped porous carbon (NC) matrix derived from MOFs is clearly obvious [27–39]. This provides a potential opportunity to study the effect of supports on performance of supported metal catalysts, even though it is not yet realized.
Herem, we designed the fabrication of nanostructured platinunnickel particles supported on nitrogen-doped porous carbons (nano-PtNi/NC), which were derived from zeolite-imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) under thermal treatment with different temperatures. For hydrosilylation of 1-octene, nano-PtNi/NC-1000 can achieve almost complete conversion and produce exclusive adduct for anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation of 1-octene. The highly efficient and selective catalysis are mainly attributed to the obvious charge transfer between nano-PtNi and nitrogen-doped porous carbon support, leading to the electronic structure of platinum species with partial charge. This catalyst also exhibits excellent reusability towards anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of nano-PtNi and nano-Pt particles
2.1 Synthesis of nano-PtNi and nano-Pt particlesNano-PtNi particles were synthesized according to the modified method reported in literature [39]. In a typical synthesis procedure, \(8.0\ \mathrm{mg\ Pt(acac)_{2}}\) (0.02 mmol), \(80.0\ \mathrm{mg\ poly(vinylpyrrolidone)}\) (PVP, \(M_{\mathrm{W}}=8,000\)), \(5.0\ \mathrm{mg\ Ni(acac)_{2}}\) (0.02 mmol) and \(50\ \mathrm{mg}\) benzoic acid were dissolved in \(8\ \mathrm{mL}\) DMF, followed by vigorous stirring for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). The resulting homogeneous green solution was transferred into a \(15\ \mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave. Then, the sealed vessel was heated at \(180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\). When the reaction solution cooled down to room temperature, the as-obtained nano-PtNi particles were precipitated by acetone, separated via centrifugation, and further purified by an ethanol-acetone mixture. The nano-Pt particles was prepared with the same synthesis condition of nano-PtNi particles except \(\mathrm{Ni(acac)_{2}}\) (0.02 mmol) was added.
## 2.2 Synthesis of ZIF-8
2.2 Synthesis of ZIF-8ZIF-8 was prepared according to the following method reported in literature [30]. In detail, \(2.38\ \mathrm{g}\) (8 mmol) \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) was dissolved in the mixed solution of \(36\ \mathrm{mL}\) DMF, \(12\ \mathrm{mL}\) ethanol and \(12\ \mathrm{mL}\) methanol. Then, the solution of \(2.627\ \mathrm{g}\) (32 mmol) 2-methyl imidazole, \(12\ \mathrm{mL}\) DMF and \(8\ \mathrm{mL}\) methanol was directly added into the above solution. After stirring for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\) at room temperature, the white powders were centrifuged, washed with methanol, and dried.
## 2.3 Synthesis of NC-600, NC-800, NC-900 and NC-1000
2.3 Synthesis of NC-600, NC-800, NC-900 and NC-1000In a typical synthesis procedure, the as-obtained ZIF-8 was transferred into a stone boat, followed by placing into a tube furnace and maintaining 600, 800, 900 and \(1{,}000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\ \mathrm{h}\) under argon atmosphere, respectively. When it naturally cooled to room temperature, the samples, NC-600, NC-800, NC-900 or NC-1000, were obtained respectively.
## 2.4  Synthesis  of  nano-PtNi/NC-600,  nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900, nano-PtNi/NC-1000 and nano-Pt/NC-1000
2.4 Synthesis of nano-PtNi/NC-600, nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900, nano-PtNi/NC-1000 and nano-Pt/NC-1000In a typical synthesis procedure, \(50\ \mathrm{mg}\) of the as-obtained NC-600, NC-800, NC-900 and NC-1000, respectively, were dispersed in \(20\ \mathrm{mL}\) ethanol and sonicated for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). \(5\ \mathrm{mL}\) ethanol dispersion of nano-PtNi \((1\ \mathrm{mg/mL})\) was added to NC-600, NC-800, NC-900 and NC-1000, respectively. Then, the mixture was stirred at room temperature for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\). The products, marked as nano-PtNi/NC-600, nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900 and nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalysts, were separated by centrifugation and dried at \(70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum, respectively. The nano-Pt/NC-1000 was prepared with the same synthesis condition of nano-PtNi/NC-1000 except nano-PtNi was replaced by \(5\ \mathrm{mL}\) ethanol dispersion of nano-Pt \((1\ \mathrm{mg/mL})\).
## 2.5 Catalytic test
2.5 Catalytic testThe catalytic performance of the as-obtained samples was evaluated by catalyzing a hydrosilylation reaction of 1-octene with \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\). To ensure the comparability of catalytic results, we used the same amount of platinum in different PtNi nanocomposites. Typically, 1-octene of \(112\ \mathrm{mg}\) (1.0 mmol), \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\) of \(213\ \mathrm{mg}\) (1.3 mmol), the as-obtained catalysts (Pt 0.0015 equiv.; \(12\ \mathrm{mg}\) of nano-PtNi/NC-600, \(11\ \mathrm{mg}\) of nano-PtNi/NC-800, \(13\ \mathrm{mg}\) of nano-PtNi/NC-900, \(15\ \mathrm{mg}\) of nano-PtNi/NC-1000, \(15\ \mathrm{mg}\) of NC-1000, \(15\ \mathrm{mg}\) of nano-Pt/NC-1000 and \(3\ \mathrm{mg}\) of commercial Pt/C catalysts, respectively), toluene of \(3\ \mathrm{mL}\) and n-dodecane of \(76\ \mathrm{mg}\) (0.45 mmol) were added into a \(10\ \mathrm{mL}\) round-bottomed flask, followed by sonicated for \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) and heated at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and stirred. In cyclic experiments, the spent catalyst was centrifugated at the end of each reaction and washed with toluene several times. Finally, the obtained catalyst directly used in the next cycle. Aliquots were taken at specific time for determination of component concentrations by GC-MS. The yield was determined by GC using n-dodecane as an internal standard.
