## 3 Results and discussion
The route for synthesis of nano-PtNi/NC catalysts was illustrated in Fig. 1(a). ZIF-8 was prepared by the in situ assembly of 2-methyl imidazole and zinc nitrate hexahydrate at room temperature, and then pyrolyzed at appropriate temperature to form a nitrogen-doped porous carbon. Finally, nanostructured platinum-nickel particles, which were prepared by solvothermal method, were loaded on the as-obtained NC supports to obtain the nano-PtNi/NC catalysts.
NC-1000 catalyst were single-crystal structure with well-defined lattice fringes. The interplanar spacing was \(0.208\;\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the (111) facets of the fcc Pt-Ni alloy (Fig. 1(d)). The atomic ratio of Pt and Ni of PtNi particles was near 1:1, as detected by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) in Table S1 in the ESM. The corresponding element maps of the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst observed by the energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis showed that C and N elements were mainly throughout the entire nano-PtNi/NC catalyst, and Pt and Ni elements are both homogeneously distributed in the nanocrystal without the significant component segregation (Fig. 1(e)). In addition, the distribution of PtNi particles in nano-PtNi/NC-600, nano-PtNi/NC-800 and nano-PtNi/NC-900 catalysts were also demonstrated in Fig. S6 in the ESM.
The hydrosilylation of 1-octene with \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\) was carried out over the as-obtained nano-PtNi/NC catalysts in toluene solution at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 2(a)). Figure 2(b) displayed the kinetic study of the reaction over nano-PtNi/NC-600, nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900 and nano-PtNi/NC-1000, respectively. As shown in Fig. 2(b), the nano-PtNi/NC-600 exhibited no obvious activity of the hydrosilylation of 1-octene with \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\). With the increase of carbonization temperature of ZIF-8, catalytic activity was detected. The nano-PtNi/NC-800 exhibited a certain activity. The nano-PtNi/NC-900 showed a yield of \(99\%\) for anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation product of 1-octene (marked as 2) after \(14\,\mathrm{~h~}\). However, the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst almost completely converted 1-octene into anti-Markovnikov hydrosilylation product 2. When the reaction time was \(4\,\mathrm{h}\), the yield of the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst \((99\%)\) was the highest than those of nano-PtNi/NC-600 \((0.5\%)\), nano-PtNi/NC-800 \((1\%)\) and nano-PtNi/NC-900 \((20\%)\) catalysts. Meanwhile, the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 displayed a great selectivity of \(99\%\), which was obviously higher than those of nano-PtNi/NC-600 (no detected), nano-PtNi/NC-800 \((83\%)\) and nano-PtNi/NC-900 \((95\%)\) catalysts (Fig. 2(c)). For comparison, the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 (Fig. S7 in the ESM), commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) and NC-1000 support were employed as the reference samples. As shown in Fig. 2(d), the kinetic curves of the reaction over nano-PtNi/NC-1000, nano-Pt/NC-1000, the commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) and NC-1000 support were performed. When the reaction time was \(4\,\mathrm{h}\), the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 exhibited yield
of \(99\%\) and selectivity of \(99\%\) for the product 2. However, the nano-Pt/NC-1000 catalyst only showed yield of \(33\%\) and selectivity of \(79\%\) for the product 2, which were much lower than that of the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst. Moreover, under the same condition, no obvious yield and selectivity of NC-1000 support and commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) were obtained (Figs. 2(d) and 2(e)).
It is shown for the first time that the modification on support of the nano-PtNi catalysts might have a great influence on performance for the hydrosilylation reaction of 1-octene with \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\). The as-prepared nano-PtNi supported on NC-1000 catalyst exhibits highest activity with \(99\%\) yield and \(99\%\) selectivity compared with other nano-PtNi/NC catalysts. We further evaluated the stability of nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst via the recyclability test. There is no obvious loss of activity even after being reused 5 times (Fig. 3(a)). The selectivity of anti-Markovnikov product 2 was close to \(99\%\) after five cycles. The results implied that the excellent stability of nano-PtNi/NC-1000. As shown in Fig. 3(b), the morphology of the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst after catalytic tests still retained. Moreover, the distribution of PtNi particles in nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst was almost unchanged after use (Fig. 3(b)).
To further understand the effect of the support modification on performance of the hydrosilylation reaction of 1-octene with \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\) over the nano-PtNi/NC catalysts, we carried out the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy(XPS). According to the XPS analysis of the as-prepared catalysts, we found the presence of nitrogen (N), carbon (C), platinum (Pt), and nickel (Ni). Table S2 in the ESM summarized the contents of total nitrogen and the contents of different kinds of nitrogen in the nano-PtNi/NC catalysts. With the increase of carbonization temperature of ZIF-8, the types and content of N species were different. From N 1s spectra, two peaks located at 398.6 and \(400.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) in nano-PtNi/NC-600 catalyst were attributed to pyridinic N and pyrrolic N (Fig. 4(a)). And, another peak at \(401.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) indexed to graphitic N was detected when the carbonization temperature of ZIF-8 was elevated over \(800\;\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figs. 4(b)–4(d)). The ratio of graphite N of nano-PtNi/NC-1000 was as high as \(52.7\%\) in three types of N species, which was about 1.5 and 4 times higher than that of \(36.8\%\) in nano-PtNi/NC-900 and \(13.3\%\) in nano-PtNi/NC-800, respectively (Table S2 in the ESM). For C 1s spectra, the peak intensity gradually became strong with the increase of pyrolysis temperature from 600 to \(1{,}000\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 4(e)). These findings suggested that higher pyrolysis temperature contributed to the higher graphitization degree, leading to stronger charge transfer between NC support and nano-PtNi.
To reveal the charge transfer from the nano-PtNi particles to NC support, the electron and oxidization states of Pt in nano-PtNi/NC catalysts were also analyzed by XPS [25, 40]. As shown in Fig. 4(f), the peak position of \(\mathrm{Pt}\,4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) in nano-PtNi/NC and nano-PtNi/NC-600 catalyst was close to that of zero valence Pt, marked as Pt (0). Pt \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) binding energies of nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900 and nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalysts gradually shifted toward higher valence compared with nano-PtNi/NC-600. And the valence of Pt species in
nano-PtNi/NC-800, nano-PtNi/NC-900 and nano-PtNi/ NC-1000 catalysts were between those of \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}(0)\), suggesting that there Pt species carried partially positive charges. These findings well confirmed electron transfer between nano-PtNi and NC supports. The more positive charges of \(\mathrm{Pt}\) in nano-PtNi particles of nano\(\mathrm{PtNi/NC-1000}\) was a possible cause of the higher catalytic activity for hydrosilylation reaction of 1-octene than that of nano-PtNi/ NC-800 and nano-PtNi/NC-900 catalysts. The peak position of Pt \(4f_{7/2}\) in nano-PtNi/NC-600 catalyst is close to \(\mathrm{Pt}(0)\), revealing the very weak charge transfer between nano-PtNi and NC-600 support, resulting in the lowest catalytic activity for hydrosilylation of 1-octene. Our findings clearly demonstrate that the NC supports obtained by carbonization of ZIF-8 at different temperatures offers an effective approach to optimize catalytic performance of hydrosilylation reaction via altering the electronic structure of nano-PtNi catalysts by enhancing electron transfer between nano-PtNi and NC supports.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully prepared nanostructured PtNi particles supported on nitrogen-doped carbon obtained by carbonization of ZIF-8 under different temperatures. Due to obvious charge transfer between nano-PtNi particles and NC-1000 support, the nano-PtNi/NC-1000 catalyst exhibited the excellent yield of \(99\%\) and outstanding selectivity of \(99\%\) as well as good reusability for the hydrosilylation reaction of 1-octene with \((\mathrm{EtO})_{3}\mathrm{SiH}\). This
work provides a new path to design and optimize electronic structure of catalysts by support modification to enhance electron transfer between metal active species and supports for highly efficient and selective catalytic performance.
